Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

I recently performed a regional analysis of differentially methylated regions using the following workflow:

#filtering coverage, normalisation, ontarget region

CH.filtered = filterByCoverage(CH, lo.count = 10, lo.perc = NULL, hi.count = NULL, hi.perc = NULL)
CH_filtered_norm = normalizeCoverage(CH.filtered)
data = read.table('path/to/region_list/file', header = FALSE)
colnames(data) = c('chr','start','end')
bed = with(data,GRanges(chr, IRanges (start,end)) )
ontarget = regionCounts(CH_filtered_norm , bed)

#methylation detection

meth_targets = unite(ontarget)
mydiff_targets = calculateDiffMeth(meth_targets)
mydiff_targets_20p = get.methylDiff(mydiff_targets, difference = 1, qvalue = 0.05)

Sample output:
chr start end strand pvalue qvalue meth.diff
chr6 25758574 25760493 * 0 0 10.19284
chr8 106832961 106833918 * 0 0 -15.129
0 0
0 0

Given the small differentially methylation difference between the treated and control group, the p and q value looks a little suspicious as I expect a little more variation between groups.

My questions are:
(1) Is it normal to have a p and q value of zero?
(2) How is regional analysis calculated? My current understanding is that the counts for Cs (methylated) and Ts (unmethylated) are summed across regions and a logistic regression is performed between stress and control groups. Is my understanding of it correct?

Thanks

